FORWARD PREDICTION, written 2026-09-17 05:4x -- *** BEFORE `RXW' AND `Rdrop' LANDED. ***
The file's own mtime is the order proof, which is this project's discriminator against a number
fitted to an answer.  At the time of writing: 14 of 18 legs landed; RXW and nox IN FLIGHT at ~36 KB
with no RESULT line; Rdrop and WW6 not yet started.

## THE SIGNATURE STRUCTURE THE TABLE HAS REVEALED (measured, all legs landed so far)

The pit rows are BYTE PAIRS behind the 8253 LSB/MSB flip-flop, and each mutant class moves a
characteristic member.  Store rows are `ps', load rows `pl', and the second member carries a `B'.

    class                   write side                     read side
    DROP                    32   LSB row only (Wdrop)      ?  -- Rdrop pending
    DUPLICATE               64   BOTH rows (WWall, Wpure)  32  B row only (RD2post, RD2z)
    SWAP                    32   (WXR, EXACT vs pred 32)   ?  -- RXW pending
    ADD-OTHER                4   pw rows (RWtail)          --

**The read and write sides are ASYMMETRIC**: a duplicated WRITE moves both members of a pair, a
duplicated READ moves only the B member.  The rows' own notes say why -- a write that lands twice
shifts the flip-flop parity for both bytes, while a duplicated read only spoils the byte the NEXT
read was going to get.

**EVERY measured pit component in this table is one of: 0, 4, 10, 32, 64.**

## THE PREDICTION

`PRETABLE.out' predicts a pit component of **53** for BOTH remaining read-side legs:

    RXW     42 legacy + 53 pit + 8 kbcmd = 103  ->  249/352
    Rdrop   58 legacy + 53 pit           = 111  ->  241/352   (marked *** LOW CONFIDENCE ***)

**53 MATCHES NO OBSERVED SIGNATURE.**  Both are read-side mutants, and every read-side leg measured
so far has a pit component of exactly 32.  So:

    PREDICTED HERE:  RXW    pit 32, not 53  ->  42 + 32 + 8 = 82   ->  **270/352**, not 249
                     Rdrop  pit 32 or 64 (a DROPPED read may or may not also spoil the B member --
                            I do not know which, and I am NOT guessing a single number for it)
                            pit 32 -> 58 + 32 = 90 -> 262/352
                            pit 64 -> 58 + 64 = 122 -> 230/352

**RXW is the sharp one and it lands within minutes.**  270 confirms the structure; 249 refutes it and
means the signature model is wrong rather than the prediction.

## WHY THIS IS RECORDED RATHER THAN HELD

`Wdrop' already missed by exactly this mechanism -- PRETABLE gave it the DUPLICATE count (64) for a
DROP mutant.  If RXW and Rdrop miss the same way, that is ONE prediction defect with three instances,
not three separate surprises, and the closing pass should report it as such.

*** THE HONEST LIMIT: I am deliberately NOT predicting a single number for `Rdrop'. ***  The write-side
DROP moves only the LSB row because the MSB was never going to receive the dropped value.  Whether a
dropped READ also spoils its B member depends on whether the flip-flop advances, which I have not
measured.  Naming both branches with their arithmetic is the honest form; picking one would be a guess
wearing a prediction's clothes.

